 Define reliability in term of evaluation technique. [1]
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f69\fs24\cf1\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033\ulc1 {\f0\cf0\ulc0\insrsid15926043 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid10619104 {\b\f0\fs26\cf0\ulc0\insrsid10619104 Reliability
\par }{\f0\fs23\cf0\ulc0\insrsid10619104 The reliability or consistency of a technique is how well it produces the }{\i\f0\fs23\cf0\ulc0\insrsid10619104 same }{\f0\fs23\cf0\ulc0\insrsid10619104 results on
\par separate occasions under the }{\i\f0\fs23\cf0\ulc0\insrsid10619104 same }{\f0\fs23\cf0\ulc0\insrsid10619104 circumstances. Different evaluation processes have
\par different degrees of reliability. For example, a carefully controlled experiment will have
\par high reliability. Another evaluator or researcher who follows exactly the same procedure
\par should get similar results. In contrast, an informal, unstructured interview will have low
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid10619104 {\f0\fs23\cf0\ulc0\insrsid10619104 reliability: it would be difficult if not impossible to repeat exactly the same discussion}{\f0\fs20\cf0\ulc0\insrsid10619104 .}{
\f0\cf0\ulc0\insrsid10619104\charrsid15926043 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f0\cf0\ulc0\insrsid15926043\charrsid15926043     }{\b\f0\cf0\ulc0\insrsid15926043\charrsid15926043 
